-- Q1573: Internal Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The cost of remediating each individual deficiency","Whether each deficiency has been communicated to management","Whether the combined deficiencies could reasonably result in an undetected material misstatement","The total number of control deficiencies identified during testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 265.A12, the auditor evaluates whether individually insignificant deficiencies, in combination, could result in a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement would not be prevented or detected. This requires assessing the interrelationship of deficiencies and whether they affect the same accounts, assertions, or processes.
Wrong (A): Remediation cost is a management consideration for prioritizing fixes, not a criterion for evaluating whether deficiencies constitute a material weakness.
Wrong (B): Communication to management is a reporting requirement under AU-C 265, not a factor in assessing the severity of combined deficiencies.
Wrong (D): The sheer number of deficiencies is less relevant than their interrelationship and the accounts or assertions they collectively affect.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1573;

-- Q1577: Internal Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate whether compensating controls adequately mitigate risks from limited segregation","Conclude that segregation is impossible and issue a scope limitation on the audit","Require the entity to hire additional personnel before completing the engagement","Ignore the segregation weakness since it is typical for entities of this size"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315.A105, in small entities where ideal segregation of duties is not achievable, the auditor should evaluate whether compensating controls such as detailed owner-manager oversight are designed and operating effectively to mitigate the resulting risks. The effectiveness depends on the owner-manager''s knowledge and evidence that reviews are substantive.
Wrong (B): Limited segregation does not constitute a scope limitation; the auditor can still obtain sufficient evidence by evaluating compensating controls.
Wrong (C): The auditor has no authority to require the entity to change its staffing structure as a condition of completing the audit.
Wrong (D): AU-C 315 requires the auditor to assess all identified risks regardless of entity size, not simply accept weaknesses as expected.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1577;

-- Q1578: Internal Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Developers may not complete their assigned programming tasks on schedule","Developers will have difficulty accessing the appropriate development environments","The entity''s hardware infrastructure could sustain physical or logical damage","Developers could make unauthorized changes to production without detection"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Granting system administrator access to application developers violates the segregation of duties between development and production environments. Under AU-C 315.A108, developers with admin access could promote untested code or modify production data without detection, undermining change management controls and application control integrity.
Wrong (A): Task completion timelines are a project management concern unrelated to the ITGC risk of granting elevated system access.
Wrong (B): Admin access provides more access, not less; developers would have easier, not harder, access to environments.
Wrong (C): Hardware damage is unrelated to the logical access control risk created by granting admin privileges to developers.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1578;

-- Q1579: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Audit the joint venture as a completely separate engagement from the client","Rely entirely on the joint venture partner''s auditor for all related matters","Evaluate the joint venture accounting, assess related risks, and confirm information access","Exclude the joint venture from scope because it is a separate legal entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 300.A2, a significant joint venture introduces complexities including the appropriate accounting method (equity, consolidation, or cost), related party considerations, and potential misstatement risks. The auditor must plan procedures to evaluate the investment and ensure adequate access to financial information.
Wrong (A): The joint venture is part of the client''s financial statements and does not require a separate engagement; it is addressed within the existing audit.
Wrong (B): Under AU-C 600, the auditor cannot simply delegate all responsibility to another auditor without performing oversight and evaluation procedures.
Wrong (D): A joint venture''s financial impact flows into the client''s statements regardless of its separate legal status, so exclusion from scope is inappropriate.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1579;

-- Q1580: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["New management has no audit impact since they were not involved in prior transactions","The auditor should automatically issue a going concern opinion for the transition","The audit fee should be reduced because new management may be more cooperative","Management changes may affect the control environment, requiring risk reassessment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A77, changes in senior management can significantly affect the control environment and tone at the top. New management may have different philosophies, risk appetites, or approaches to financial reporting, requiring the auditor to reassess financial statement level risks and evaluate new management''s integrity and competence.
Wrong (A): New management can affect the control environment and financial reporting policies even for prior-period transactions, making this assertion incorrect.
Wrong (B): A management change alone does not trigger an automatic going concern opinion; going concern requires evaluation of specific financial conditions under AU-C 570.
Wrong (C): Audit fees are determined by engagement complexity and risk, not by management''s cooperativeness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1580;

-- Q1581: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Asset portability, physical control adequacy, and employee access with concealment ability","Only the total value of assets reported on the balance sheet date","Only cash and cash equivalents since other assets cannot be misappropriated","Only whether the entity maintains insurance coverage for potential asset losses"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 240.A34, assessing misappropriation risk requires evaluating the nature and portability of assets (cash, inventory, and easily convertible items carry higher risk), the adequacy of physical safeguards and reconciliation procedures, and whether inadequate segregation of duties provides employees with both access to assets and the ability to conceal theft.
Wrong (B): Balance sheet value alone ignores critical factors like portability, physical controls, and segregation of duties that determine susceptibility to theft.
Wrong (C): Inventory, supplies, and equipment can all be misappropriated; limiting the assessment to cash ignores significant theft risks.
Wrong (D): Insurance coverage mitigates the financial impact of losses but does not reduce the risk of misappropriation occurring.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1581;

-- Q1582: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the cash account balances denominated in foreign currencies","Only transactions occurring in the last month of the fiscal year","Translation, remeasurement, exchange rate selection, and hedge accounting treatments","Only the accounts receivable balances denominated in foreign currencies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Significant foreign currency transactions introduce risks related to the proper application of ASC 830, including selecting appropriate exchange rates for translation and remeasurement, correctly classifying gains and losses in income or other comprehensive income, and evaluating hedge accounting designations. These areas involve significant judgment and complex requirements.
Wrong (A): Foreign currency risk extends far beyond cash to include receivables, payables, investments, and intercompany balances denominated in foreign currencies.
Wrong (B): Foreign currency transactions occur throughout the year, and limiting assessment to the last month would miss significant exposures in earlier periods.
Wrong (D): Like cash, accounts receivable is only one of many accounts affected by foreign currency risk.$EXPL$
